# Review: CallsPlugin

The live call stack is built and renders well in stories. `CallManager` joins a room through the
swarm synchronizer and the Cloudflare SFU peer, exposes self, users, tracks and media as atoms,
and toggles media. `ParticipantGrid`, the participant tile (mute, raised hand, speaking, local pin),
the toolbar, `CallArticle` and `CallDebugPanel` (with `@peermetrics/webrtc-stats`) all exist with
stories. An "Active Call" deck companion appears while joined. `SetSimulatedParticipants` is a real
operation.

The persistent half of the spec is not built. There is no `Call` ECHO type: `CallsCapabilities.Call`
is a runtime `{ roomId }`. There is no `CallTransport` and no `provisionCall`, and the transport
provider has `join(roomId)`/`leave()` with no `makeConfig`, so F-9, F-10.2–10.4, T-10, T-13 and
QA-2 are missing. Two other gaps: `CallsCapabilities.EventHandler` is a singleton that plugin-calls
never invokes (plugin-meeting's `call-extension.ts` says so), so F-5.2–5.5 are missing; and there is
no display-name gate, so F-1.3, F-6 and T-4 are missing.

The biggest divergence is the thread boundary. plugin-thread depends on `@dxos/plugin-calls` and
renders "Start video call" in its own `ChannelArticle`. It does not come from a graph
type-extension. The data types (`Participant`, `TrackBundle`, `CallState`, `MediaState`, track
ids) are shaped by the `UserState` proto and the SFU wire format, not by the spec's model. The
call "ops" are `CallManager` methods, as the spec's QA preamble itself records.

Only `plugin.test.ts` (plugin loads) is a unit test; every acceptance scenario is `missing`.

Of 82 items: 7 done, 15 untested, 3 partial, 27 diverged, 30 missing.

